  <!-- ========== 3. DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>An IRGC drone struck a UAE-flagged oil tanker in the Strait of Hormuz on August 15, according to US CENTCOM (Reuters, August 15). The vessel, carrying approximately 900,000 barrels of Abu Dhabi condensate, sustained a hull breach and was diverted to Fujairah under Emirati coast guard escort; two crew members were injured. Iran's IRGC issued no formal claim of responsibility, though IRNA described the incident as a response to continued US military presence in Iranian territorial waters. The attack is the fourth confirmed maritime incident in the strait since the MOU's collapse on July 8. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Israeli Air Force strikes on Beirut's southern suburbs on August 15 killed eleven people and destroyed a residential compound identified by the IDF as a Radwan Force command node, according to Al Jazeera and Times of Israel. A Radwan Force field commander named by IDF spokesperson Daniel Hagari as Khalil Khreiss was killed in the strikes — the highest-profile Hezbollah casualty since the Twelve-Day War ceasefire. Lebanon's Health Ministry confirmed eleven dead and twenty-three wounded. This was the deadliest single-day Israel-Lebanon exchange since late June. → supports <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>US President Trump declared on August 14 that Hormuz is "an American waterway" and that the US reserves the right to take any action to keep it open, in a statement published on Truth Social and confirmed by the White House press office to Reuters. Iran's Foreign Ministry issued a counter-statement within hours calling the declaration "legally null and in violation of international law." No US naval action followed as of August 16; the US Fifth Fleet had not changed its rules of engagement, according to unnamed CENTCOM officials cited by Reuters. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>OFAC General License X, which authorized energy-sector transactions with Iranian counterparties as part of the June 22 Switzerland roadmap's sanctions relief tranche, expires on August 21 with no renewal instrument yet published to the Federal Register as of August 16. The license covers approximately $2.1 billion in monthly Iranian oil export revenues channeled through non-sanctioned third parties, according to Bloomberg (August 14). Named US Treasury officials contacted by Bloomberg declined to comment on renewal status. No White House statement on the waiver's future has been made as of August 16. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>Iran's Foreign Ministry reiterated on August 12 that IAEA inspectors have not been granted access to Fordow or Natanz since the June 22 Switzerland talks concluded, and that resumed access remains contingent on a final binding agreement — not a roadmap. IAEA Deputy Director General for Safeguards Massimo Aparo told Reuters on August 14 that the agency's monitoring capabilities in Iran remain significantly degraded and that provisional arrangements from the June 22 framework have not been operationalized. No IAEA press release has confirmed any site visits since June. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:40%;">Escalation 40%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:40%;">Protracted 40%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:20%;">De-esc 20%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (40%):</strong> F5 at 48% is the primary driver: a direct Israeli airstrike on Iranian soil within 45 days is roughly a coin-flip, and the Hormuz drone incident plus Trump's territorial declaration have removed the informal restraint norms that held June 17-July 8; add F2 (Hormuz framework) resolving NO (78% likely) and the combined pathway to escalation is the single most probable scenario.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (40%):</strong> F1 (sanctions waiver) renewal and continued Oman-channel communications without a breakthrough produce a frozen-conflict dynamic: Hormuz remains 80-90% closed, Lebanon exchanges continue below all-out war threshold, and the October 27 Israeli election approaches without a regional deal.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (20%):</strong> Requires F1 (waiver renewed) AND F2 (Hormuz framework) both resolving YES — a combined probability of roughly 0.45 × 0.22 ≈ 10% — plus IAEA inspection access resuming (F3 at 28%), a package that implies a confidence-building deal has been agreed outside the public record.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Press TV coverage on August 14-15 characterized Trump's Hormuz declaration as a violation of UNCLOS Articles 38 and 45, with Iranian maritime operations framed as lawful exercises of sovereign rights. Kayhan's August 15 editorial described the US statement as imperial overreach and called on the government to maintain PGSA permit authority over all strait transits. No Iranian state outlet acknowledged the August 15 UAE tanker attack directly in official coverage — the incident appeared only through unnamed IRGC-affiliated Telegram accounts as a "defensive operation in the occupied waters." → supports <span class="cite">F2</span></p>
      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei's last confirmed public appearance remains June 8; no statement from the Supreme Leader's office has addressed the IAEA's August 14 comments on inspection access or the Hormuz situation directly. Press TV cited unnamed senior IRGC officials confirming that Iran's 60-percent enrichment program at Fordow remains fully operational. Tasnim ran commentary attributing the June MOU collapse to US bad faith in allowing Israel to continue Lebanon operations during the ceasefire window — that framing appeared three times in state outlet headlines between August 12-15. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
      <p>Araghchi's August 8 conditions statement was covered on IRNA as Iran defending its sovereign rights in negotiations — without naming the specific conditions publicly. Fars described the Oman channel as on hold by Iranian initiative, without acknowledging the July 8 MOU collapse. Kayhan's editorial staff called for a full suspension of all negotiations pending the August 21 sanctions waiver decision, characterizing any renewal as a US concession Iran should not accept cheaply. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">F1</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

